<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prithviraj Chauhan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1166–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Prithviraja III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prithviraja III, known as Prithviraj <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a>, was a king of the Chahamana dynasty of Shakambhari, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. He fought the Chandelas and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> at the first Battle of Tarain in 1191, but was defeated and killed after the second battle in 1192, opening northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to Ghurid conquest. He became a hero of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan.jpg" alt="Prithviraj Chauhan" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Posthumous_painting_depicting_Prithviraj_Chauhan_from_Kota_(colour).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Few inscriptions survive from Prithviraj's reign, and none was issued by the king himself. Much of what is known comes from medieval chronicles that are not entirely reliable. Prithviraja Vijaya is the only surviving literary text from his reign, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-raso">Prithviraj Raso</a>, which popularised him as a great king, contains many exaggerated accounts. Prithviraj was the son of the Chahamana king Someshvara and Karpuradevi, a Kalachuri princess, and was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The year 1166 was calculated by Dasharatha Sharma from the astrological positions given in Prithviraja Vijaya. When Someshvara died in 1177, Prithviraj was about eleven years old and ascended the throne with his mother as regent, assisted by a council and the chief minister Kadambavasa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His first military success was the suppression of a revolt by his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjuna">Nagarjuna</a> and the recapture of Gudapura, possibly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurugram">Gurgaon</a>. Madanpur inscriptions of 1182 claim that he laid waste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jejakabhukti">Jejakabhukti</a>, the Chandela kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramardi">Paramardi</a>. Before 1187 he made peace with Bhima II, the Chaulukya king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-raso">Prithviraj Raso</a> tells that he eloped with Samyogita, daughter of the Gahadavala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra</a>, but no other source supports this, and the Gahadavala records are silent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> in 1175 and was defeated by the Chaulukyas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1178. He then conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and moved his base to Punjab, which brought him into conflict with Prithviraj. Leading a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kings, Prithviraj defeated the Ghurid army near Taraori in 1191. In 1192 Muhammad returned with an army of Turkish mounted archers and defeated the Rajput army on the same battlefield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Most medieval sources say Prithviraj was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, where Muhammad planned to keep him as a Ghurid vassal, and was later killed for rebelling. Accounts of his end differ: Hasan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> says he was caught conspiring and beheaded, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammira-mahakavya">Hammira Mahakavya</a> says he refused food and died in captivity. Muhammad installed the Chahamana prince Govindaraja at Ajmer.</p><p>Later portrayals differ. Jain stories of the 14th and 15th centuries, such as the Prabandha-Chintamani, present him as an inept king responsible for his own downfall, whereas the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-raso">Prithviraj Raso</a>, patronised largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> courts, makes him a great hero. The defeat at Tarain is seen as a landmark in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the Ghurid victory was presented as a milestone leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prithviraj Chauhan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1191 a Rajput coalition under <b>Prithviraj Chauhan</b> defeated him at the First Battle of Tarain, but the next year, at the Second Battle of Tarain, his mobile cavalry won a decisive victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Prithviraj Chauhan</b> (Prithviraja III) of the Chahamanas of Shakambhari fell to Muhammad of Ghor in 1192, his son Govindaraja was installed as a Ghurid vassal and moved to the fortress of Ranthambore (Ranastambhapura) in eastern Rajasthan.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-ranastambhapura">Chahamanas of Ranastambhapura</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prithviraja III</b> defeated Muhammad of Ghor at the first battle of Tarain in 1191, but lost the second in 1192 and was executed.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prithviraja III</b> of the Chahamanas of Shakambhari defeated Paramardi in 1182, and in 1203 Qutb al-Din Aibak took Kalinjar.</blockquote><a href="/state/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.47,74.64&amp;year=1182" aria-label="Ajmer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Prithviraj Chauhan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6109 5231h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6109 5231h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.47,74.64&amp;year=1182">What was here in 1182? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
